<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Embedded Footprint</title>
<style>
:root { color-scheme: light dark; }
body { font-family: sans-serif; margin: 0; }
table { border-collapse: collapse; }
th, td { border: 1px solid #8888; padding: 0.25em 0.5em; }
td:nth-child(n+4) { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<p>The code, data, and stack sizes in bytes of one filter step: one prediction and one update, per filter configuration, update form policy, and optimization level, for version @VERSION@. Each step is linked alone, without any library, for a bare-metal Arm Cortex-M4 target with its single-precision floating-point unit, with @COMPILER@: <code>@FLAGS@</code>. The flash memory holds the <code>.text</code>, <code>.rodata</code>, and <code>.data</code> sections, the random-access memory the <code>.data</code> and <code>.bss</code> sections. The filter is statically allocated. The stack is the worst case of the step compiled as one function: a build that does not inline the library calls may use more or less. The configurations are in <a href="https://github.com/FrancoisCarouge/Kalman/tree/master/benchmark/footprint" target="_top">benchmark/footprint</a>.</p>
<p>The simple form is the smallest but the least numerically robust: its estimate uncertainty may lose its symmetry and positive definiteness. The Joseph form preserves them, at a higher cost.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Configuration</th><th>Policy</th><th>Optimization</th><th>.text</th><th>.rodata</th><th>.data</th><th>.bss</th><th>Flash</th><th>RAM</th><th>Stack</th></tr>
@TABLE@</table>
</body>
</html>
